# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF endings.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true — the default on Git for Windows, and common in WSL —
# a checkout rewrites these with CRLF, and bash then fails on the very first
# line of the file:
#
#   handoff.sh: line 20: $'\r': command not found
#   handoff.sh: line 21: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# Reported from a real WSL machine. "text eol=lf" overrides autocrlf for these
# paths, so the working tree gets LF no matter how the user has git configured.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Python entry points carry a shebang and are executed directly.
*.py text eol=lf

# Config and docs: normalised in the repository, LF in the working tree, so a
# template's line endings cannot vary by who cloned it.
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Everything else: let git decide, but never guess at binaries.
* text=auto
